  "output": "I've reviewed the full codebase. It's honest to say up front: this is a very early-stage Next.js app (a landing page, auth, and one working console page — Sources). There's no real user-facing data volume yet, so several of the categories you asked about (N+1 queries, re-render storms, memory leaks) don't have live instances to point at. Rather than manufacture problems, here's what's actually there, plus the couple of real footguns worth fixing now before they compound.\n\n## 1. N+1 query patterns\n**None currently** — the whole app makes exactly one query (`listSources()` in `lib/server/sources.ts:19`). But there's a **latent redundant-fetch pattern** worth fixing before more pages land:\n\n`lib/server/sources.ts:24` — `countSourcesBySide()` calls `listSources()` internally, which re-runs the full DB query and re-validates every row through `sourceSchema.parse()`. Any page that needs both the list *and* the counts (e.g. a future dashboard) will hit the database twice and double the Zod parsing for no reason.\n\n```ts\n// lib/server/sources.ts — before\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const all = await listSources(); // separate DB round-trip\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nFix: wrap the underlying fetch in React's `cache()` so repeated calls within one request dedupe automatically (this is the standard Next.js App Router pattern for this exact problem):\n\n```ts\nimport { cache } from \"react\";\n\nexport const listSources = cache(async (): Promise<Source[]> => {\n  await connection();\n  const rows = await getDb().select().from(sources).orderBy(sources.createdAt);\n  return rows.map((row) => sourceSchema.parse(row));\n});\n```\n\nThat alone makes `countSourcesBySide()` free when called alongside `listSources()` in the same request — no query, no re-parse.\n\nAlso worth pre-empting given the schema: `runs`, `matches`, and `conflicts` all have unbounded foreign-key relationships (`entities.runId`, `matches.entityAId/entityBId`, `conflicts.matchId`). When the Runs/Conflicts pages get built, the natural first draft is \"fetch matches, then loop and fetch conflicts per match\" — that's exactly where N+1 will show up. Flag now: those pages should use Drizzle's relational query API (`db.query.matches.findMany({ with: { conflicts: true } })`) or explicit joins, not per-row fetches.\n\n## 2. Unnecessary React re-renders\nAlso essentially none — almost everything is a Server Component by design (the code comment in `sources.ts` is explicit: \"TanStack Query is reserved for run-status polling only\"). The only client components are `nav-rail.tsx`, `landing-nav.tsx`, `sign-out-control.tsx`, `step-list.tsx`, `pipeline-strip.tsx` — all small, none with derived-state or expensive render work, so memoization (`useMemo`/`React.memo`) would be premature here.\n\nOne thing to watch for when the run-status polling gets built: TanStack Query re-renders the whole subscribing component on every poll tick. When you build that, scope the query to the smallest component (e.g. a `<RunStatusBadge runId>` rather than polling at the page level) so a status tick doesn't re-render the surrounding page.\n\n## 3. Caching opportunities\n- **React `cache()` around `listSources()`** — see #1, the concrete win available today.\n- `getDb()` (`lib/db/index.ts:27`) already does the right thing — connection cached on `globalThis`, lazy init, `prepare: false` for the Supabase pooler. No change needed there.\n- No `unstable_cache`/ISR opportunity for `sources` — correctly, since the code comment explains sources are deliberately request-time dynamic (`connection()` call), and that's a real constraint (config can change between requests), not an oversight. Don't add revalidation caching against that.\n- When `runs`/`conflicts` pages arrive, those are much better ISR/`unstable_cache` candidates than `sources` — a finished run's data is immutable (audit trail is append-only), so per-run pages can be cached indefinitely and invalidated only via tag on status change.\n\n## 4. Memory leaks\nNone found. No `setInterval`/`setTimeout` without cleanup, no manual `addEventListener`, no unclosed subscriptions. `getDb()`'s `globalThis` caching is the correct pattern for avoiding connection-pool leaks across Next.js dev HMR reloads — worth calling out as *already handled correctly*, not a bug.\n\n## 5. Redundant computations\n- `countSourcesBySide()` (`lib/server/sources.ts:23-30`) does three separate `.filter()` passes over the same array. Trivial at current scale, but if you'd rather compute it in one pass:\n\n```ts\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const all = await listSources();\n  return all.reduce(\n    (acc, s) => {\n      if (s.kind === \"salesforce\" || s.kind === \"hubspot\") acc.crm++;\n      else if (s.kind === \"databricks\" || s.kind === \"snowflake\") acc.warehouse++;\n      else if (s.kind === \"synthetic\") acc.synthetic++;\n      return acc;\n    },\n    { crm: 0, warehouse: 0, synthetic: 0 },\n  );\n}\n```\nHonestly not worth it at table sizes this small (sources is a config table, not entity data) — I'd skip this one unless it's called on a hot path.\n\n## Bottom line\nThe only change I'd actually make right now is wrapping `listSources()` in `cache()` — it's a one-line fix that prevents duplicate DB round-trips once a second consumer (a dashboard, a count widget) shows up, and costs nothing. Everything else on your list is premature for the app's current size; the schema comments show the team is already thinking about the right invariants (append-only audit log, immutable rulesets, engine/web write ownership) for when data volume does show up.\n",
